                  - Tx success fraction[TxT] )
	~	1/year
	~	The rate at which people with Rx OUD and past year heroin use exit \
		treatment before remitting (i.e., "drop out"), based on the average \
		duration of treatment for each MOUD, which determines the likelihood of \
		success in treatment.
	|

Tx exit with UD Rx OUD no H[TxT]  = 
        Rx OUD no heroin by MOUD[TxT] 
             * Tx exit with UD rate Rx OUD no H[TxT]
	~	person/Years
	~		|

Tx exit with UD Rx OUD with H[TxT]  = 
        Rx OUD with heroin by MOUD[TxT] 
             * Tx exit with UD rate Rx OUD with H[TxT]
	~	person/Years
	~		|

Tx exit with UD total[TxT]  = 
        Tx exit with UD HUD[TxT] 
             + Tx exit with UD Rx OUD no H[TxT] 
             + Tx exit with UD Rx OUD with H[TxT]
	~	people/year
	~		|

Tx intake capacity[TxT]  = 
        Max ( 0, 
             DELAY1 ( Tx exit total[TxT] , 
                  Tx intake delay net[TxT] ) 
                  + ( Tx capacity effective[TxT] 
                       - Total by MOUD[TxT] ) 
                       / Tx intake delay net[TxT] )
	~	people/year
	~	How many people can be intaken to each treatment type at any given point \
		in time, which increases with effective (not simply theoretical) capacity, \
		and is limited both by people currently in treatment and intake delays. \
		(2.15)
	|

Tx intake delay=
	0.298
	~	year [0,?]
	~	[LIT] Delay between contacting and entering treatment, among those who \
		receive treatment. [Source: Flavin 2020, Beetham 2019, Lucas 2010, \
		Kawasaki 2020, Madden 2018, Gryczynski 2009 for bup; Madden 2018, \
		Gryczynski 2009, Lucas 2010 for MMT] Delay roughly same for all. [Canadian \
		source: Canada's Drug and Health Technology Agency (CADTH) 2019 \
		environmental scan of opioid use disorder treatment programs in Canada; \
		mean of reported wait times]
	|

Tx seeking fraction MMT HUD relative  = 0.8
	~	dmnl [0.5,1]
	~	Not used in the Canadian model (Vivitrol is not modelled, so the MMT \
		share is 1 - Bup share). US model: fraction of HUDs not seeking Bup who \
		seek MMT rather than Vivitrol. [Source: Dr. Alex Walley, personal \
		communication, 7/1/20, Dr. Ben Bearnot, personal communication 7/1/20]
	|

Tx seeking fraction MMT Rx OUD relative=
	0.15
	~	dmnl
	~	Not used in the Canadian model (Vivitrol is not modelled, so the MMT \
		share is 1 - Bup share). US model: [EXP, MCAL] Fraction of Rx OUDs not \
		seeking Bup nor Vivitrol, scaled to 0-1. Fraction of all people in methadone with Rx OUD vs HUD is highly \
		sensitive to this value so it was manually calibrated to ensure that Rx \
		OUD patients were the minority, as consistent with TEDS admissions. \
		[Source: Dr. Alex Walley, personal communication 7/1/20, TEDS 2002]
	|

Tx seeking rate HUD relative to Rx OUD no H=
	4.843
	~	dmnl [4.85,10,0.05]
	~	[DATA] Rate at which individuals with HUD seek MOUD treatment relative to \
		that rate in individuals with Rx OUD. This is based on estimates from \
		NSDUH 2019 of the fraction of people with Rx OUD or HUD in MOUD treatment, \
		which for individuals with HUD is 4.84 times that of Rx OUD. This is \
		consistent with both TEDS and prior NSDUH data that show significantly \
		higher rates of treatment engagement from individuals with HUD vs OUD. \
		Because that data is based on receipt rather than seeking, this assumes \
		that the barriers faced by Rx OUD vs HUD do not differ in their relative \
		magnitude or effect. This assumption is also made in the barrier variables \
		in the model. [Source: NSDUH 2019]
	|

Tx seeking rate Rx OUD no H total net  = 
        Tx seeking rate Rx OUD no H total 
             * ( 1
                  + RAMP ( Policy change Tx seeking rate Rx OUD no H total 
                            / Policy rampup duration , 
                       Policy activation time , 
                       Policy activation time 
                            + Policy rampup duration ) )
	~	1/year
	~	The rate at which individuals with Rx opioid use disorder and no heroin \
		use seek MOUD treatment, after accounting for policy changes.
	|

Tx success fraction lambda=
	2
	~	1/year [0,?]
	~	[EXP, LIT, MCAL] Lambda value calibrated to match distribution based on \
		expert estimates. [Source: Drs. Ben Bearnot, Keith Humphreys, John Kelly, \
		and Kate McHugh; Eastwood 2017]
	|

Tx success fraction inflection=
	2.5
	~	Years [0,?]
	~	[MCAL, EXP] Inflection point (M) value calibrated to match distribution \
		based on expert estimates
	|

Tx success fraction kappa=
	2.5
	~	dmnl [0,2]
	~	[MCAL, EXP] Kappa value calibrated to match distribution based on expert \
		estimates
	|

Tx success fraction max=
	0.85
	~	dmnl
	~	[EXP] Max possible fraction of treatment that ends "successfully" ie into \
		remission rather than back into UD.
	|

TxT:
	Bup,MMT
	~	
	~		|

Tx average duration net[TxT]  = 
        Tx average duration[TxT] 
             * ( 1
                  + RAMP ( Policy change Tx average duration 
                            / Policy rampup duration , 
                       Policy activation time , 
                       Policy activation time 
                            + Policy rampup duration ) )
	~	year
	~	Average duration in years per episode for each type of MOUD treatment, \
		after accounting for policy changes.
	|

********************************************************
	.calibration
********************************************************~
	|

SimVar[RMis]=
	PWURx NP no PY heroin/unit people ~~|
SimVar[ROUD]=
	Rx OUD no PY heroin total/unit people ~~|
SimVar[ROUH]=
	Rx OUD with PY heroin total/unit people ~~|
SimVar[NDHU]=
	Nondisordered heroin use/unit people ~~|
SimVar[HUD]=
	HUD total/unit people ~~|
SimVar[TxBp]=
	Total by MOUD[Bup]/unit people ~~|
SimVar[TxMt]=
	Total by MOUD[MMT]/unit people ~~|
SimVar[ODRB]=
	Total overdose deaths base Rx/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[ODHC]=
	Total overdose deaths heroin/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[ODSB]=
	Total overdose deaths synth base/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InRM]=
	Initiating Rx NP own Rx/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InRD]=
	Initiating Rx NP diverted/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InHT]=
	Total heroin initiation/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InHD]=
	Initiating heroin no Rx/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InHR]=
	Initiating heroin with Rx NP/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[InHO]=
	Initiating heroin with Rx OUD/unit people per year ~~|
SimVar[PrElm]=
	:NA: ~~|
SimVar[ODTo]=
	Total overdose deaths/unit people per year
	~	dmnl
	~	Subscripts cannot have more than one unit, which leads to unit errors when \
		working with multiple data streams. To address this, we standardized the \
		units for calibration to prevent unit errors and allow us to focus on \
		identifying and resolving actual model errors.
	|

DataPriorBase[StElm]=
	:NA: ~~|
DataPriorBase[FlElm]=
	:NA: ~~|
